{"issues":[{"expand":"renderedFields,names,schema,operations,editmeta,changelog,versionedRepresentations","id":"10037","self":"https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/rest/api/latest/issue/10037","key":"AP-3","fields":{"summary":"Team & Velocity Configuration","issuetype":{"self":"https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/rest/api/2/issuetype/10037","id":"10037","description":"Stories track functionality or features expressed as user goals.","iconUrl":"https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/rest/api/2/universal_avatar/view/type/issuetype/avatar/10315?size=medium","name":"Story","subtask":false,"avatarId":10315,"entityId":"a9502e0d-867b-4e13-9c45-261e65fe910f","hierarchyLevel":0},"parent":{"id":"10033","key":"AP-1","self":"https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/rest/api/2/issue/10033","fields":{"summary":"Core Scheduling Engine MVP","status":{"self":"https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/rest/api/2/status/10066","description":"","iconUrl":"https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/","name":"To Do","id":"10066","statusCategory":{"self":"https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/rest/api/2/statuscategory/2","id":2,"key":"new","colorName":"blue-gray","name":"To Do"}},"priority":{"self":"https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/rest/api/2/priority/1","iconUrl":"https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/images/icons/priorities/highest_new.svg","name":"Highest","id":"1"},"issuetype":{"self":"https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/rest/api/2/issuetype/10035","id":"10035","description":"Epics track collections of related bugs, stories, and tasks.","iconUrl":"https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/rest/api/2/universal_avatar/view/type/issuetype/avatar/10307?size=medium","name":"Epic","subtask":false,"avatarId":10307,"entityId":"21b28705-2e2b-43f3-95af-c2d445b6d5a2","hierarchyLevel":1}}},"creator":{"self":"https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/rest/api/2/user?accountId=712020%3A87eb1729-fc17-4029-97ee-3780fbdbe2e9","accountId":"712020:87eb1729-fc17-4029-97ee-3780fbdbe2e9","emailAddress":"ataiassistant@gmail.com","avatarUrls":{"48x48":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1e948a9d865d5779585b350f8f9a0c0c?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FAA-2.png","24x24":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1e948a9d865d5779585b350f8f9a0c0c?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FAA-2.png","16x16":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1e948a9d865d5779585b350f8f9a0c0c?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FAA-2.png","32x32":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/1e948a9d865d5779585b350f8f9a0c0c?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FAA-2.png"},"displayName":"AI Assistant","active":true,"timeZone":"Europe/Minsk","accountType":"atlassian"},"components":[],"created":"2025-12-10T14:11:15.587+0300","description":"h2. Overview\r\n\r\n*Purpose*: This solution design document describes the architecture and implementation approach for adding Team & Velocity Configuration functionality to the Gantt Schedule Calculation System. This feature enables project managers to configure team capacity parameters (velocity and project start date) through a configuration file, providing the foundational configuration needed for accurate schedule calculations.\r\n\r\n*Usage*: This document provides a structured approach for documenting the solution architecture and breaking down the development scope into manageable tasks.\r\n\r\n----\r\n\r\nh2. Business Context\r\n\r\nh3. Problem Statement\r\n\r\n{quote}\r\nProject managers need a way to configure their team's velocity and project start date so that the Gantt schedule calculation system can accurately reflect actual team capacity and project timelines. Currently, these parameters are either hardcoded or not configurable, limiting the system's flexibility and accuracy for different projects and teams.\r\n{quote}\r\n\r\nh3. Business Objectives\r\n\r\n*Objective 1*: Enable project managers to define team velocity and project start date through a simple configuration file\r\n*Objective 2*: Ensure configuration validation prevents invalid or missing critical parameters\r\n*Objective 3*: Provide clear error messages when configuration is invalid to facilitate quick troubleshooting\r\n\r\nh3. Success Criteria\r\n\r\n*Criterion 1*: System successfully loads and validates configuration from config.json file\r\n*Criterion 2*: All required configuration fields (projectStartDate, sprintDurationDays, velocity) are accessible to the scheduler component\r\n*Criterion 3*: System provides clear, actionable error messages for all validation failure scenarios\r\n\r\n----\r\n\r\nh2. Solution Architecture\r\n\r\nh3. High-Level Architecture\r\n\r\n{code:markdown}\r\n```mermaid\r\ngraph TB\r\n    subgraph \"Configuration Layer\"\r\n        A[config.json File]\r\n        B[Config Loader]\r\n        C[Config Validator]\r\n        D[Config Singleton/Context]\r\n    end\r\n    \r\n    subgraph \"Scheduling Layer\"\r\n        E[Scheduler Component]\r\n        F[Schedule Calculator]\r\n    end\r\n    \r\n    A --> B\r\n    B --> C\r\n    C -->|Valid| D\r\n    C -->|Invalid| G[Error Handler]\r\n    D --> E\r\n    E --> F\r\n    G --> H[User-Friendly Error Messages]\r\n```\r\n{code}\r\n\r\n*Description*: The high-level architecture introduces a Configuration Layer that reads and validates the config.json file. The validated configuration is stored in a singleton or context object that is accessible to the Scheduler Component. The configuration flow ensures that only valid configurations reach the scheduling engine, with clear error handling for invalid inputs.\r\n\r\nh3. Component Architecture\r\n\r\n{code:markdown}\r\n```mermaid\r\nflowchart TD\r\n    Start([Application Start]) --> LoadConfig[Load config.json]\r\n    LoadConfig --> ParseJSON{Parse JSON}\r\n    ParseJSON -->|Success| ValidateFields{Validate Required Fields}\r\n    ParseJSON -->|Error| JSONError[Handle JSON Parse Error]\r\n    ValidateFields -->|All Present| ValidateTypes{Validate Field Types}\r\n    ValidateFields -->|Missing| MissingFieldError[Error: Missing Required Field]\r\n    ValidateTypes -->|Valid Date| ValidateNumbers{Validate Numeric Values}\r\n    ValidateTypes -->|Invalid Date| DateError[Error: Invalid Date Format]\r\n    ValidateNumbers -->|Positive Values| StoreConfig[Store in Singleton/Context]\r\n    ValidateNumbers -->|Zero/Negative| NumberError[Error: Must be Positive]\r\n    StoreConfig --> Available[Config Available to Scheduler]\r\n    Available --> End([Ready for Scheduling])\r\n    JSONError --> End\r\n    MissingFieldError --> End\r\n    DateError --> End\r\n    NumberError --> End\r\n```\r\n{code}\r\n\r\n*Description*: The component architecture details the configuration loading and validation workflow. The system first attempts to parse the JSON file, then validates that all required fields are present, followed by type validation (ISO date format for projectStartDate, positive numbers for sprintDurationDays and velocity). Only after all validations pass is the configuration stored and made available to the scheduler component.\r\n\r\nh3. Data Architecture\r\n\r\n{code:markdown}\r\n```mermaid\r\nerDiagram\r\n    CONFIG_FILE ||--|| CONFIG_OBJECT : contains\r\n    CONFIG_OBJECT ||--|| SCHEDULER : provides\r\n    CONFIG_OBJECT {\r\n        string projectStartDate \"ISO Date Format\"\r\n        number sprintDurationDays \"Positive Integer\"\r\n        number velocity \"Positive Number\"\r\n    }\r\n    SCHEDULER {\r\n        date startDate \"From config\"\r\n        number sprintDays \"From config\"\r\n        number pointsPerSprint \"From config\"\r\n    }\r\n```\r\n{code}\r\n\r\n*Description*: The data architecture shows the relationship between the configuration file, the configuration object, and the scheduler. The CONFIG_OBJECT contains the three required fields (projectStartDate as ISO date string, sprintDurationDays and velocity as positive numbers) and provides these values to the SCHEDULER component for schedule calculations.\r\n\r\nh3. Integration Architecture\r\n\r\n{code:markdown}\r\n```mermaid\r\nsequenceDiagram\r\n    participant App as Application\r\n    participant Loader as Config Loader\r\n    participant Validator as Config Validator\r\n    participant Context as Config Context/Singleton\r\n    participant Scheduler as Scheduler Component\r\n    \r\n    App->>Loader: Initialize Application\r\n    Loader->>Loader: Read config.json from file system\r\n    Loader->>Validator: Parse and Validate JSON\r\n    Validator->>Validator: Check Required Fields\r\n    Validator->>Validator: Validate projectStartDate (ISO format)\r\n    Validator->>Validator: Validate sprintDurationDays (> 0)\r\n    Validator->>Validator: Validate velocity (> 0)\r\n    alt Validation Success\r\n        Validator->>Context: Store Validated Config\r\n        Context-->>App: Configuration Ready\r\n        App->>Scheduler: Access Configuration\r\n        Scheduler->>Context: Get projectStartDate\r\n        Scheduler->>Context: Get sprintDurationDays\r\n        Scheduler->>Context: Get velocity\r\n        Context-->>Scheduler: Return Config Values\r\n    else Validation Failure\r\n        Validator-->>App: Error with Details\r\n        App->>App: Display Error Message\r\n    end\r\n```\r\n{code}\r\n\r\n*Description*: The integration architecture shows the sequence of interactions during configuration loading. The Application initializes the Config Loader, which reads the file and passes it to the Validator. The Validator performs all required checks, and on success, stores the configuration in a Context/Singleton object. The Scheduler Component can then access the configuration values as needed for schedule calculations.\r\n\r\n----\r\n\r\nh2. Technical Design\r\n\r\nh3. Technology Stack\r\n\r\n*Runtime & Language:*\r\n* Node.js - Runtime environment for the CLI application\r\n* TypeScript - Type-safe development with compile-time validation\r\n\r\n*Libraries & Frameworks:*\r\n* fs (Node.js built-in) - File system operations for reading config.json\r\n* JSON parsing (Node.js built-in) - Native JSON parsing capabilities\r\n* TypeScript interfaces - Type safety for configuration structure\r\n\r\n*Tools & Infrastructure:*\r\n* TypeScript Compiler - Type checking and compilation\r\n* Vitest - Unit testing framework for validation logic\r\n* ESLint - Code quality and consistency\r\n\r\nh3. Design Patterns\r\n\r\n*Pattern 1: Singleton Pattern* - The configuration object will be implemented as a singleton to ensure a single source of truth and prevent accidental modifications after initialization. This pattern ensures consistent access across the application.\r\n\r\n*Pattern 2: Validation Pattern* - A dedicated validation layer will check all configuration values before they are stored, ensuring data integrity and providing clear error messages for invalid inputs.\r\n\r\n*Pattern 3: Immutable Configuration* - Once loaded and validated, the configuration object will be immutable to prevent runtime modifications that could lead to inconsistent scheduling calculations.\r\n\r\nh3. Security Considerations\r\n\r\n*Security measure 1*: File path validation to prevent directory traversal attacks when reading config.json from user-specified paths\r\n*Security measure 2*: JSON parsing error handling to prevent application crashes from malformed configuration files\r\n*Security measure 3*: Input sanitization for date strings to prevent injection attacks (though minimal risk in this context)\r\n\r\nh3. Performance Considerations\r\n\r\n*Performance optimization 1*: Configuration loading occurs once during application initialization, minimizing runtime overhead\r\n*Performance optimization 2*: Lazy validation - validate only when configuration is accessed, if reload capability is implemented\r\n*Performance optimization 3*: Caching validated configuration in memory to avoid repeated file reads\r\n\r\nh3. Scalability Considerations\r\n\r\n*Scalability approach 1*: The singleton pattern ensures efficient memory usage with a single configuration instance\r\n*Scalability approach 2*: Support for configuration file path parameterization allows for different configurations per project/environment without code changes\r\n\r\n----\r\n\r\nh2. Development Scope Decomposition\r\n\r\nh3. Decomposition Strategy\r\n\r\n*Important: This scope is manageable as a single implementation unit. The configuration loading, validation, and singleton pattern implementation are tightly coupled and should be developed together. No decomposition into separate components is needed.*\r\n\r\n*Approach: Single Solution Design*\r\n\r\nThe configuration system is a cohesive unit that includes:\r\n* Configuration file reading\r\n* JSON parsing and validation\r\n* Singleton/context pattern implementation\r\n* Error handling and messaging\r\n\r\nThese components work together and cannot be meaningfully separated without creating artificial boundaries that would add unnecessary complexity.\r\n\r\n----\r\n\r\nh3. Component: Configuration System\r\n\r\n*Scope*: This component covers the complete configuration loading, validation, and access functionality. It includes file reading, JSON parsing, field validation (presence, type, and value constraints), singleton/context implementation, and comprehensive error handling.\r\n\r\n*Task 1.1: Configuration File Reader and Parser*\r\n\r\n*Acceptance Criteria:*\r\n* AC1: System reads config.json from specified path (default: project root)\r\n* AC2: System handles file not found errors gracefully with clear error messages\r\n* AC3: System parses valid JSON format and handles JSON parse errors with descriptive messages\r\n* AC4: System supports configurable file path via command-line argument or environment variable\r\n\r\n*Dependencies*: None (foundational task)\r\n\r\n*Estimated Effort*: 2 story points\r\n\r\n*Task 1.2: Configuration Schema and Type Definitions*\r\n\r\n*Acceptance Criteria:*\r\n* AC1: TypeScript interface defined for configuration structure with projectStartDate (string), sprintDurationDays (number), and velocity (number)\r\n* AC2: Interface includes JSDoc comments documenting field requirements and formats\r\n* AC3: Type definitions are exported and reusable across the application\r\n\r\n*Dependencies*: None (can be developed in parallel with Task 1.1)\r\n\r\n*Estimated Effort*: 1 story point\r\n\r\n*Task 1.3: Configuration Validation Logic*\r\n\r\n*Acceptance Criteria:*\r\n* AC1: Validator checks for presence of all required fields (projectStartDate, sprintDurationDays, velocity)\r\n* AC2: Validator validates projectStartDate is a valid ISO date string format (e.g., \"2024-01-01\")\r\n* AC3: Validator validates sprintDurationDays is a positive number (> 0)\r\n* AC4: Validator validates velocity is a positive number (> 0)\r\n* AC5: Validator provides specific error messages indicating which field(s) are invalid and why\r\n* AC6: All validation scenarios are covered by unit tests\r\n\r\n*Dependencies*: Task 1.2 (requires type definitions)\r\n\r\n*Estimated Effort*: 3 story points\r\n\r\n*Task 1.4: Configuration Singleton/Context Implementation*\r\n\r\n*Acceptance Criteria:*\r\n* AC1: Configuration is stored in a singleton instance or dependency injection context\r\n* AC2: Configuration object is immutable after initialization (read-only access)\r\n* AC3: Configuration is accessible to scheduler component through defined interface\r\n* AC4: Attempting to modify configuration after initialization throws error or is prevented\r\n* AC5: Configuration loading occurs during application initialization\r\n\r\n*Dependencies*: Task 1.1, Task 1.2, Task 1.3 (requires all previous components)\r\n\r\n*Estimated Effort*: 2 story points\r\n\r\n*Task 1.5: Error Handling and User Messaging*\r\n\r\n*Acceptance Criteria:*\r\n* AC1: All error scenarios provide clear, actionable error messages\r\n* AC2: Error messages indicate which configuration field is invalid\r\n* AC3: Error messages provide guidance on expected format (e.g., \"projectStartDate must be in ISO format: YYYY-MM-DD\")\r\n* AC4: Configuration loading errors are logged appropriately for debugging\r\n* AC5: System provides clear feedback when configuration is successfully loaded\r\n\r\n*Dependencies*: Task 1.3 (error handling integrated with validation)\r\n\r\n*Estimated Effort*: 2 story points\r\n\r\n*Task 1.6: Integration with Scheduler Component*\r\n\r\n*Acceptance Criteria:*\r\n* AC1: Scheduler component can access projectStartDate from configuration\r\n* AC2: Scheduler component can access sprintDurationDays from configuration\r\n* AC3: Scheduler component can access velocity from configuration\r\n* AC4: Scheduler uses configuration values for schedule calculations\r\n* AC5: Integration is tested with valid configuration scenarios\r\n\r\n*Dependencies*: Task 1.4 (requires singleton/context to be implemented)\r\n\r\n*Estimated Effort*: 2 story points\r\n\r\n----\r\n\r\nh2. Development Workflow\r\n\r\n{code:markdown}\r\n```mermaid\r\ngraph LR\r\n    A[Planning] --> B[Design]\r\n    B --> C[Development]\r\n    C --> D[Code Review]\r\n    D --> E[Testing]\r\n    E --> F{Pass?}\r\n    F -->|Yes| G[Integration]\r\n    F -->|No| C\r\n    G --> H[Documentation]\r\n    H --> I[Deploy]\r\n```\r\n{code}\r\n\r\n*Description*: The development workflow follows a standard agile process: Planning and Design phase to understand requirements, Development of configuration system components, Code Review for quality assurance, comprehensive Testing (unit tests for validation logic, integration tests for scheduler access), Integration with existing scheduler component, Documentation updates, and final Deployment.\r\n\r\n----\r\n\r\nh2. Dependencies & Risks\r\n\r\nh3. Dependencies\r\n\r\n*External Dependency 1*: File system access permissions - Impact if delayed: Configuration file cannot be read, blocking application startup\r\n*External Dependency 2*: Scheduler component interface - Impact if delayed: Configuration values cannot be consumed by scheduler, limiting functionality\r\n*Internal Dependency 1*: Existing configuration types structure - Impact if delayed: May need to extend or refactor existing config/types.ts to accommodate new fields\r\n\r\nh3. Risks & Mitigation\r\n\r\n*Risk 1*: Configuration file path resolution issues across different operating systems\r\n*Probability*: Medium\r\n*Impact*: Medium\r\n*Mitigation*: Use Node.js path module for cross-platform path handling, provide clear documentation on expected file locations\r\n\r\n*Risk 2*: Date parsing inconsistencies with different ISO date formats\r\n*Probability*: Low\r\n*Impact*: Medium\r\n*Mitigation*: Use strict ISO date validation (YYYY-MM-DD format), provide clear examples in error messages\r\n\r\n*Risk 3*: Configuration reload requirements not addressed in initial implementation\r\n*Probability*: Medium\r\n*Impact*: Low\r\n*Mitigation*: Design singleton pattern to support future reload capability, document as optional enhancement\r\n\r\n----\r\n\r\nh2. Testing Strategy\r\n\r\nh3. Unit Testing\r\n\r\n*Coverage target*: 90% for configuration validation logic\r\n*Key areas*: \r\n* Field presence validation\r\n* Date format validation\r\n* Numeric value validation (positive numbers)\r\n* Error message generation\r\n* Singleton/context access patterns\r\n\r\nh3. Integration Testing\r\n\r\n*Scope*: Test configuration loading and access by scheduler component\r\n*Key scenarios*:\r\n* Successful configuration load and scheduler access\r\n* Configuration file not found scenario\r\n* Invalid JSON format scenario\r\n* Missing required field scenarios\r\n* Invalid field value scenarios\r\n\r\nh3. End-to-End Testing\r\n\r\n*Scope*: Test complete flow from application startup to scheduler using configuration\r\n*Key user journeys*:\r\n* Application starts with valid config.json, scheduler uses configuration values\r\n* Application starts with invalid config.json, appropriate error is displayed\r\n* Application starts without config.json, appropriate error is displayed\r\n\r\n----\r\n\r\nh2. Deployment Plan\r\n\r\nh3. Environments\r\n\r\n*Development*: Local development with config.json in project root\r\n*Staging*: Staging environment with test configuration files\r\n*Production*: Production environment with validated configuration files\r\n\r\nh3. Deployment Strategy\r\n\r\n*Approach*: Direct deployment - configuration system is part of the application codebase\r\n*Rollback plan*: Revert to previous version if configuration loading causes application startup failures\r\n*Monitoring*: Monitor application startup logs for configuration loading errors, track configuration validation failures\r\n\r\n----\r\n\r\nh2. Documentation Requirements\r\n\r\n*API Documentation*: Document configuration interface, singleton/context access methods, and validation error types\r\n*User Documentation*: Document config.json format, required fields, field formats, example configurations, and common error scenarios with solutions\r\n*Technical Documentation*: Document configuration loading flow, validation rules, singleton pattern implementation, and integration points with scheduler\r\n*Runbooks*: Document troubleshooting steps for common configuration issues (file not found, invalid format, missing fields)\r\n\r\n----\r\n\r\nh2. Timeline & Milestones\r\n\r\n*Milestone 1*: Configuration file reading and parsing - Week 1\r\n*Milestone 2*: Validation logic and error handling - Week 1-2\r\n*Milestone 3*: Singleton/context implementation and scheduler integration - Week 2\r\n\r\n----\r\n\r\nh2. Open Questions\r\n\r\n*Question 1*: Should the system support multiple configuration files with priority order (e.g., project-specific overrides default)? - *Owner*: Product Owner - *Status*: Deferred to future enhancement\r\n*Question 2*: Should configuration reloading be supported without application restart? - *Owner*: Technical Lead - *Status*: Optional enhancement, not in initial scope\r\n*Question 3*: What is the expected behavior if config.json is missing? Should the application fail to start or use default values? - *Owner*: Product Owner - *Status*: Application should fail with clear error message (required fields must be provided)\r\n\r\n----\r\n\r\nh2. Appendix\r\n\r\nh3. References\r\n\r\n*[Reference 1]*: Solution Design Template - https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/AC/pages/25133058/Solution+design+template\r\n*[Reference 2]*: JIRA Story Template - https://kidblendapp.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/AC/pages/786465/JIRA+Template+Story\r\n*[Reference 3]*: Existing Configuration Types - src/config/types.ts\r\n\r\nh3. Glossary\r\n\r\n*Configuration Singleton*: A single instance of the configuration object that is accessible throughout the application, ensuring consistency and preventing accidental modifications\r\n*ISO Date Format*: International standard date format (YYYY-MM-DD) used for projectStartDate field\r\n*Velocity*: Story points per sprint, representing team capacity for work completion\r\n*Sprint Duration*: Number of days in a single sprint cycle\r\n\r\n----\r\n\r\nh2. Sample Configuration\r\n\r\n{noformat}\r\n{\r\n  \"projectStartDate\": \"2024-02-01\",\r\n  \"sprintDurationDays\": 10,\r\n  \"velocity\": 20\r\n}\r\n{noformat}\r\n\r\n----\r\n\r\nh2. Story Acceptance Criteria Coverage\r\n\r\n*AC1*: Given a valid config.json file with all required fields, When the system loads the configuration, Then all fields are accessible to the scheduler component.\r\n*Coverage*: Task 1.4 (Singleton/Context Implementation) and Task 1.6 (Scheduler Integration)\r\n\r\n*AC2*: Given a config.json file with missing projectStartDate field, When the system attempts to load the configuration, Then an error is thrown indicating the missing required field.\r\n*Coverage*: Task 1.3 (Validation Logic) and Task 1.5 (Error Handling)\r\n\r\n*AC3*: Given a config.json file with invalid projectStartDate format (e.g., \"inva